A||||8||TwisT||3.4 aftermarket;  is there such a thing???||||1||
Z||000000||TwisT||04-24-2000||09:36 AM||||Hey guys,  considering an engine swap at the tail-end of this summer.  I really love the idea of a 3800SC,  but I also realize that the 3.4 is a much easier swap (as close to a bolt in without actually sticking in another doggy 2.8).<P>My question is what sort of aftermarket (or factory) performance accessories can we get for the 3.4?  I haven't seen any,  aside from the standard ignition/  exhaust stuff.||||reg||1
Z||000001||mrfixit58||04-24-2000||10:32 AM||||The next best place to get this kind of info is your local GM dealer, parts man. He will have a cataloge that lists camshafts and other performance goodies available through GM. You can also talk to Crane Cams, Competition Cams, etc. Of course, the best source is the guys on this list (O.K. guys, it's time to show your stuff).<P>I too, have lusted over the 3800 SC conversion. Unfortunatly, the total price tag for the conversion is a little hard to swallow. The 3.4 with a performance cam, high flow cat and muffler, and hogged out exhaust manifolds should easily reach 200+ horsepower. <P>If you shop around, you can get a 3.4 block for under $500. If your handy, you can drill the block for the starter yourself or for a small fee, try a local machine shop. Everything else "Fiero" will bolt on. Plug in a set of injectors from a 305 Chebby and your ready to go. In taking this route, I figure for around $1000, you'll wind up with a 50-60 horsepower gain and a permanent smile.<P>Good Luck,<BR>Roy||||reg||1
Z||000002||Greg Piet||04-24-2000||01:31 PM||||OK I was talking to guy who works at Chevy at my Last SCCA Race.  He told me there is a goodwrench motor 3.4litre, that is a direct replacement for the 60 degree 2.8 litre for $2400 US.<P>I figure hes not full of it, as it was just a friendly chat and not for braging purposes||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||04-24-2000||03:51 PM||||Those new GM 3.4 crate engines still need to have the starter moved to the other side. I've seen a few dealerships sell them as low as $1800 or $1900, actually not a bad deal.||||reg||1
Z||000004||MooCow||04-24-2000||05:22 PM||||Hey if you wanted to you can contact Ed Parks from the Fiero factory like I'm doing.  Just email him fierofactory@juno.com and I believe his price is about $2500 for a really low milage ready to bolt up 3.4.<BR>              <A HREF="http://www.welcome.to/thefierofactory" TARGET=_blank>http://www.welcome.to/thefierofactory</A>                     <BR>                                  Thanks Ken||||reg||3
Z||000005||TwisT||04-24-2000||07:59 PM||||Curious,  I know there's a million low-milage 3.4's out there just sitting in salvage yards waiting for a new home.  With GM putting so many of these motors in their vehicles,  you'd think the aftermarket would be a healthy one.  I'll have to do some Internet surfing tonight to see what I can find...||||reg||1
Z||000006||WisconsinGT||04-24-2000||10:11 PM||||I too am in the market for a 3.4. I was under the impression that if you have an auto Fiero that you have to shave off some of the 3.4 block to make it fit. No?||||reg||1
Z||000007||batboy||04-24-2000||11:01 PM||||My understanding is that these GM 3.4 crate engines were overruns from mid-90 Camaro and Firebirds. GM apparently thought lots more people would buy these cars with the V6 option than did. I guess everyone wanted V8's instead! Supplies are probably limited. Actually, except for Fiero and S-10 owners, there's not a big demand for the 3.4 pushrod motor as an upgrade. They were only used for a few years in a few cars, so the salvage yards aren't exactly full of 3.4 engines laying around. What about the newer front wheel drive 3.4 in the 1999 and 2000 Grand Am/Grand Prix? I wonder if those will fit?||||reg||1
Z||000008||loafer87gt||04-24-2000||11:23 PM||||Hey guys,<P>My local Gm dealer gave me a kickass price for a new GM 3.4 Performance crate engine. I was given the price $2200 CDN for the kit. This is a hell of a deal as a stock 2.8 replacement motor costs $1750 CDN. The name of the place is Sherwood Chevrolet and their phone number is 364-6330. I have the guys name written down and the exact price on a business card. unfortunately, the card is lost in my desk somewhere, I'll post the info as soon as I find the card. ||||reg||1
